The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
@Test
public void nonSecureTest() {
    // Non-secure test logic
}

@Test
public void secureTest() {
    // Secure test logic that requires authentication
}
```
Reason:
The non-secure test may alter the authentication context, causing the subsequent secure test to fail due to it not being in an appropriate authentication state.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
// Separate secure tests into their own module
public class SecureTestModule {
    @Test
    public void secureTest() {
        // Secure test logic that requires authentication
    }
}
```
Reason:
By isolating secure tests into their own module, it ensures that they run in a separate JVM and do not get impacted by the execution of non-secure tests, maintaining the integrity of the authentication context required for secure testing.

